From f0139e0000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH 0139e] evergo: stop invalidating pages when enforcement is off

The v63 boot got all the way to launchd sending the validated shared cache
and then dyld died:

  Sending validated cache to launchd
  Trying to send bytes to launchd
  dyld[241]: Assertion failed: (_mapper), Function SharedCache,
      File ProcessFlags.cpp, (line 888)
  CRASH xpcproxy[241] sig 6 sub 0x0

The same screen shows why:

  CODE SIGNING: cs_invalid_page(0x102a90000): p=23[apfscomp]
      final status 0x22000000, allowing (remove VALID) page

The shared cache shards are hard links, and without a trust cache their pages
can never validate. vm_fault() checks VMP_CS_TAINTED() *before* it checks
whether enforcement is enabled (vm_fault.c: the tainted branch comes first,
the "!cs_enforcement_enabled" branch comes later), so every tainted page still
reaches cs_invalid_page(), which then strips CS_VALID and complains - and
dyld, which asked for those pages, asserts.

With enforcement off there is no trust cache, so no Apple-signed page can ever
validate and every one of them is "tainted". Tearing CS_VALID off the process
for that is not enforcement, it is just noise - and it is what breaks dyld.

Skip the invalidation entirely when enforcement is off: the page stays
untouched, cs_invalid_page() returns 0, and dyld gets its cache.
---
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_cs.c b/bsd/kern/kern_cs.c
index 43b1ea6..40d36d3 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_cs.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_cs.c
@@ -254,6 +254,26 @@ cs_invalid_page(addr64_t vaddr, boolean_t *cs_killed)
 
 	p = current_proc();
 
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+	/*
+	 * evergo (0139e): with code-signing enforcement off there is no trust
+	 * cache, so no Apple-signed page can ever validate and every page looks
+	 * tainted. vm_fault() checks VMP_CS_TAINTED() before it checks whether
+	 * enforcement is on, so all of them land here; stripping CS_VALID then
+	 * breaks dyld, which asserts in SharedCache::_mapper as soon as the
+	 * shared cache pages are invalidated.
+	 *
+	 * There is nothing to enforce and nothing to report - let the page
+	 * through untouched.
+	 */
+	if (!cs_system_enforcement() && !cs_process_enforcement(p)) {
+		if (cs_killed) {
+			*cs_killed = FALSE;
+		}
+		return 0;
+	}
+#endif
+
 	if (verbose) {
 		printf("CODE SIGNING: cs_invalid_page(0x%llx): p=%d[%s]\n",
 		    vaddr, proc_getpid(p), p->p_comm);
